On Sat, Oct 11, 2014 at 4:46 PM, 'John E. Clifford' via lojban <
lojban@googlegroups.com> wrote:

> Does p have to be the same for each value of x?  Or does it just have to
> be the same up to x?  That is, can this be "I want three men to run for
> governor" in the sense of, say, needing to fill out a ballot.  They have to
> be this worldly, but my wishes don't involve their identities. ?
>

p is the proposition that satisfies "ci da zo'u ke'a du'u da co'e", so yes
with singular "ci" it has to be the same proposition for each of three
values of x, which doesn't make much sense.

Now with plural "ci" it would make sense again, since now it is, for some
three men, the proposition that they run.

mu'o mi'e xorxes

